  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nEarly this Monday morning the weakening nor'easter is located\njust south of Long Island NY. The center of the storm, while\ncontinuing to weaken will pass northeast through the Gulf of\nMaine tonight.\n\nThe main effects from this storm from here on out are bands of\nlocally heavy rain rotating in from off the ocean through this\nevening. So far the bands seem rather narrow so not everyone\nwill see heavy rain. Otherwise, clouds, drizzle and a raw\nnortheast breeze will continue with gusts continuing to\ndecrease.\n\nHave extended the high surf advisory through this afternoon as\nan astronomically high tide around midday combines with large\nswell. This will increase the risk for dangerous rip currents as\nwell as potentially causing additional beach erosion. Some minor\nsplashover remains possible around the time of high tide today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAfter the nor'easter moves offshore, the 500mb pattern looks to\nfavor the development of a ridge over the eastern US. With high\npressure setting up south of New England, moderating \ntemperatures are expected for mid to late week with mainly dry \nconditions. Some weak instability could allow for a stray \nshower to generate on Wednesday or Thursday, but most should \nremain dry. \n\nRain chances are a little higher on Friday, as a cold front is \npoised to move southward across New England. However rain \nchances are likely to remain in the slight-chance category, as \nthe front remains weak and any instability will have to \novercome a modest subsidence layer. High pressure then looks to \ndominate the weather over the weekend.",
